1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,043
God, that is the most beautiful

2
00:00:02,085 --> 00:00:04,463
engagement ring ever.

3
00:00:04,505 --> 00:00:05,965
Yeah, well, you should know.

4
00:00:06,005 --> 00:00:09,176
You've bought, like,
a billion of them.

5
00:00:09,217 --> 00:00:10,928
Yeah, you didn't get one.

6
00:00:12,554 --> 00:00:14,515
Okay. Well,
tonight's the big night.

7
00:00:14,556 --> 00:00:16,015
Hey, listen, how you
going to ask her?

8
00:00:16,057 --> 00:00:17,476
It is going to be perfect.

9
00:00:17,518 --> 00:00:19,436
I am taking her to her
favorite restaurant.

10
00:00:19,478 --> 00:00:21,229
I'm going to get her
a bottle of the champagne

11
00:00:21,271 --> 00:00:24,817
that she really loves therefore
knows how expensive it is.

12
00:00:26,401 --> 00:00:28,111
Then, when the glasses are full

13
00:00:28,153 --> 00:00:31,072
instead of proposing a toast,
I'm just going to propose.

14
00:00:31,114 --> 00:00:32,741
Oh-h.

15
00:00:32,783 --> 00:00:34,785
- It sounds perfect.
- Yeah.

16
00:00:34,827 --> 00:00:37,705
You're going to mess it up.
Let me do it.

17
00:00:37,746 --> 00:00:38,914
I'm not going to mess it up.

18
00:00:38,956 --> 00:00:41,834
If she says no,
can I have the ring?

19
00:00:41,876 --> 00:00:43,418
She's not going to say no.

20
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:44,628
If.

21
00:00:47,172 --> 00:00:49,925
- Hey.
- Hey.

22
00:00:49,967 --> 00:00:51,510
[muffled]
Hi, Monica.

23
00:00:54,388 --> 00:00:56,390
- Give it.
- It's gone.

24
00:00:57,725 --> 00:00:58,767
Phoebe.

25
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:04,732
[muffled]
Hey, Monica.

26
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:08,903
We're practically kissing.

27
00:01:11,321 --> 00:01:14,241
[theme music]

28
00:01:16,410 --> 00:01:21,331
♪ So no one told you
life was gonna be this way ♪

29
00:01:21,373 --> 00:01:23,625
♪ Your job's a joke
you're broke ♪

30
00:01:23,667 --> 00:01:26,378
♪ Your love life's DOA ♪

31
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:30,925
♪ It's like you're always
stuck in second gear ♪

32
00:01:30,966 --> 00:01:33,385
♪ When it hasn't been
your day your week ♪

33
00:01:33,427 --> 00:01:35,930
♪ Your month
or even your year ♪

34
00:01:35,971 --> 00:01:39,850
♪ But I'll be there for you ♪

35
00:01:39,892 --> 00:01:44,730
♪ 'Cause you're there
for me too ♪♪

36
00:01:48,776 --> 00:01:50,110
- Hey.
- Hey.

37
00:01:50,151 --> 00:01:51,904
Are any of you guys
free tonight?

38
00:01:51,946 --> 00:01:53,739
My boss is hosting
this charity event

39
00:01:53,781 --> 00:01:55,282
for underprivileged kids

40
00:01:55,323 --> 00:01:57,492
and the more people I bring,
the better I look.

41
00:01:57,534 --> 00:01:59,160
So, Monica, Chandler.

42
00:01:59,202 --> 00:02:00,746
Well, Monica
and Chandler can't go.

43
00:02:00,788 --> 00:02:02,414
We're going to dinner,
remember?

44
00:02:02,456 --> 00:02:04,249
Oh, my God, I'm so sorry.

45
00:02:04,291 --> 00:02:06,460
What's the big deal?

46
00:02:06,501 --> 00:02:08,087
I just get mad when

47
00:02:08,127 --> 00:02:10,255
Rachel doesn't remember
where we're going.

48
00:02:12,758 --> 00:02:14,676
Where are you going?

49
00:02:16,386 --> 00:02:18,889
- How about you guys?
- Open bar?

50
00:02:18,931 --> 00:02:20,223
I think so.

51
00:02:20,265 --> 00:02:22,810
I can do that for the kids.

52
00:02:22,851 --> 00:02:24,269
Yeah, you know what?
I'll come, too.

53
00:02:24,311 --> 00:02:25,646
I'm making money now.

54
00:02:25,687 --> 00:02:27,188
It's about time
I give something back.

55
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:31,110
Well, you could also give back
the money you owe me.

56
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:33,403
Okay...have a benefit.

57
00:02:34,738 --> 00:02:36,406
Hey.

58
00:02:36,448 --> 00:02:38,075
Hey, Ross,
listen, can you

59
00:02:38,117 --> 00:02:39,409
come to a charity event tonight?

60
00:02:39,451 --> 00:02:41,536
Oh, no, I have plans
with Elizabeth.

61
00:02:41,578 --> 00:02:43,789
Oh, so you're already
doing your part for the kids.

62
00:02:46,458 --> 00:02:48,335
Sorry, it's just one
of our last nights together

63
00:02:48,376 --> 00:02:52,840
before she leaves for camp
to be a counselor.

64
00:02:52,881 --> 00:02:54,466
Ross, let me ask
you a question.

65
00:02:54,508 --> 00:02:57,719
All jokes aside, where is
this relationship going?

66
00:02:57,761 --> 00:02:59,138
Wait a minute.
All jokes aside?

67
00:02:59,178 --> 00:03:02,307
I didn't agree to that.

68
00:03:02,349 --> 00:03:04,476
Do you really see this
as a long-term thing?

69
00:03:04,518 --> 00:03:05,894
I don't know.

70
00:03:05,936 --> 00:03:08,271
You know, you are
12 years older than her.

71
00:03:08,313 --> 00:03:10,816
Wait a minute, d-does everyone
feel this way?

72
00:03:10,858 --> 00:03:12,109
- Yeah.
- Sort of.

73
00:03:12,151 --> 00:03:13,152
- Yeah,
- Sorry.

74
00:03:13,192 --> 00:03:14,528
Oh, wow, uh.

75
00:03:14,569 --> 00:03:16,363
I thought you guys
were like just making jokes.

76
00:03:16,404 --> 00:03:18,073
I had no idea.

77
00:03:18,115 --> 00:03:19,741
Well, you know what?
You guys are wrong.

78
00:03:19,783 --> 00:03:22,703
Yes, there is a chronological
age difference

79
00:03:22,744 --> 00:03:24,287
but I never notice it.

80
00:03:24,329 --> 00:03:26,832
You know why?
Because she is very mature.

81
00:03:26,874 --> 00:03:27,958
Besides, it doesn't
really matter to me

82
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,459
what you guys think.

83
00:03:29,501 --> 00:03:31,545
I mean I'm the one
dating Elizabeth, not you.

84
00:03:31,586 --> 00:03:33,547
Not what she said last night.

85
00:03:36,175 --> 00:03:38,969
Yeah. See, now,
he could date her.

86
00:03:42,764 --> 00:03:44,683
Will you marry me?

87
00:03:45,684 --> 00:03:47,686
Will you marry me?

88
00:03:47,728 --> 00:03:50,147
Hey, you marry me.

89
00:03:56,945 --> 00:03:59,865
What's going on,
Little Elves?

90
00:03:59,907 --> 00:04:01,992
It's the big night.
We wanted to wish you good luck.

91
00:04:02,034 --> 00:04:03,284
Yeah, yeah. You
have the ring?

92
00:04:03,326 --> 00:04:04,619
Yeah, right here
in my pocket.

93
00:04:04,661 --> 00:04:06,454
Oh.

94
00:04:09,583 --> 00:04:11,668
- Pheebs.
- What? Oh. O-op.

95
00:04:13,795 --> 00:04:15,422
Okay, now, will you
guys get out of here?

96
00:04:15,463 --> 00:04:16,548
I want this to
be a surprise

97
00:04:16,590 --> 00:04:17,925
and she's
going to know.

98
00:04:17,966 --> 00:04:20,427
Yeah, yeah, you guys,
get out of here.

99
00:04:22,179 --> 00:04:24,056
Hey. Hi, guys.

100
00:04:26,265 --> 00:04:28,769
- You look beautiful.
- Mm. Thank you.

101
00:04:35,650 --> 00:04:37,736
What's going on?

102
00:04:37,778 --> 00:04:39,613
We're just really ex..

103
00:04:39,654 --> 00:04:41,406
...excited about this
charity event

104
00:04:41,448 --> 00:04:44,034
that we have to go to.

105
00:04:48,455 --> 00:04:50,289
- Here.
- Oh, thank you.

106
00:04:50,331 --> 00:04:52,292
So, now, what's
going on here?

107
00:04:52,333 --> 00:04:54,878
Uh, well, uh, this
is a silent auction.

108
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:56,713
They lay out
all the stuff here

109
00:04:56,755 --> 00:04:58,548
and then you write
down your offer

110
00:04:58,590 --> 00:05:00,425
and then the highest bid
gets it.

111
00:05:00,467 --> 00:05:02,552
No, I know what
a silent auction is.

112
00:05:02,594 --> 00:05:04,846
I meant, what's going
on with your hair?

113
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:08,183
Uh, w-why?

114
00:05:08,225 --> 00:05:09,893
No, it's nice.

115
00:05:11,436 --> 00:05:13,188
- Nice to see you, Rachel.
- Oh.

116
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:14,606
So glad you brought someone.

117
00:05:14,648 --> 00:05:16,524
Oh, someone?
I brought people.

118
00:05:16,566 --> 00:05:18,110
Mr. Thompson,
this is Phoebe.

119
00:05:18,152 --> 00:05:19,444
Phoebe, this is Mr. Thompson.

120
00:05:19,486 --> 00:05:21,320
He's the head
of my department.

121
00:05:21,362 --> 00:05:22,656
And I also
brought my friend Joey.

122
00:05:22,697 --> 00:05:25,284
Oh, shrimp toast.

123
00:05:26,785 --> 00:05:30,371
Uh, you know,
I-I don't know where he is.

124
00:05:30,413 --> 00:05:32,207
Well, I hope you're going to bid
on some things, Rachel.

125
00:05:32,249 --> 00:05:33,875
Well, you know what,
actually I was about to bid

126
00:05:33,917 --> 00:05:36,003
on this lovely trip to Paris.

127
00:05:36,044 --> 00:05:37,545
- Oh, nice choice.
- Yeah.

128
00:05:37,587 --> 00:05:39,673
- Good luck.
- Thank you.

129
00:05:39,714 --> 00:05:42,301
Ha. Okay.

130
00:05:42,341 --> 00:05:44,636
Twenty dollars.

131
00:05:52,686 --> 00:05:54,855
Shut the door, shut the door.

132
00:05:54,896 --> 00:05:56,648
W-What's going on?

133
00:05:56,690 --> 00:05:58,859
The guys across the hall are
throwing water balloons at us.

134
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:00,359
Oh, you have to call the police.

135
00:06:00,401 --> 00:06:03,113
That's what I did
to the kids in my building.

136
00:06:03,155 --> 00:06:05,115
No, it's a water
balloon fight.

137
00:06:05,157 --> 00:06:08,576
- We started it.
- Oh. Um...mmm.

138
00:06:08,618 --> 00:06:10,829
Listen, um, I,
I-I just stopped by

139
00:06:10,871 --> 00:06:12,956
to see if you wanted
to see this play tonight.

140
00:06:12,998 --> 00:06:14,333
Um...it's supposed
to be excellent.

141
00:06:14,373 --> 00:06:15,583
The director is--

142
00:06:15,625 --> 00:06:17,002
Who drank all
the kamikazes?

143
00:06:17,044 --> 00:06:18,377
Nobody. We put 'em in here!

144
00:06:18,419 --> 00:06:20,172
[squealing]

145
00:06:20,214 --> 00:06:21,756
- You want some?
- No.

146
00:06:21,798 --> 00:06:23,591
Okay, okay. Okay.

147
00:06:23,633 --> 00:06:25,969
Okay. Look,
can I, can I just

148
00:06:26,011 --> 00:06:27,470
just talk to you
for a second?

149
00:06:27,512 --> 00:06:28,555
Yeah, sure.

150
00:06:28,596 --> 00:06:29,890
Um, so this play, um

151
00:06:29,931 --> 00:06:31,183
what do you think?

152
00:06:31,225 --> 00:06:32,684
I-It's gotten great reviews,
you know the--

153
00:06:32,726 --> 00:06:34,393
Attack!

154
00:06:34,435 --> 00:06:35,520
[girls screaming]

155
00:06:35,562 --> 00:06:36,855
Put your balloons down.

156
00:06:36,897 --> 00:06:38,148
You put your balloons down.

157
00:06:38,190 --> 00:06:39,607
You put yours..

158
00:06:39,649 --> 00:06:43,987
Everybody! Put their
balloons down!

159
00:06:45,697 --> 00:06:48,367
Now, this is a nice suit.

160
00:06:51,661 --> 00:06:55,332
Our next item is the romantic
trip to Paris.

161
00:06:55,374 --> 00:07:00,212
It goes to Emil Alexander,
with a high bid of $2,300.

162
00:07:00,254 --> 00:07:02,505
Oh. So close.

163
00:07:03,965 --> 00:07:05,424
Heya, guys, look.

164
00:07:05,466 --> 00:07:07,969
I got me some drinks.

165
00:07:08,011 --> 00:07:09,721
What are you doing?

166
00:07:09,763 --> 00:07:12,224
Open bar.

167
00:07:12,266 --> 00:07:14,684
And finally our biggest item
of the night.

168
00:07:14,726 --> 00:07:18,188
The 22-foot gentleman's
daysailer sailboat.

169
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:21,983
The winning bid
was a whopping $20,000.

170
00:07:22,025 --> 00:07:23,318
O-oh.

171
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:25,153
I won.

172
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:29,199
That was my guess.

173
00:07:29,241 --> 00:07:30,367
What? What? What?

174
00:07:30,409 --> 00:07:32,911
I guessed $20,000.

175
00:07:32,953 --> 00:07:34,996
Joey, it is an auction.

176
00:07:35,038 --> 00:07:38,166
You don't guess,
you buy.

177
00:07:38,208 --> 00:07:39,793
What?

178
00:07:41,502 --> 00:07:43,046
I don't have $20,000.

179
00:07:43,088 --> 00:07:46,507
Congratulations on
your new boat, Joey Tribbiani.

180
00:07:50,387 --> 00:07:52,555
Joey, sit down.

181
00:07:52,597 --> 00:07:54,433
Forget her.
You enjoy this.

182
00:07:54,474 --> 00:07:56,601
Wow. Wow.

183
00:08:10,365 --> 00:08:12,700
[sighing]

184
00:08:12,742 --> 00:08:14,995
What are you doing?

185
00:08:15,036 --> 00:08:19,333
One nation, under God

186
00:08:19,374 --> 00:08:21,960
indivisible with liberty
and justice for all.

187
00:08:22,002 --> 00:08:23,378
[chuckling]
I remembered it.

188
00:08:23,420 --> 00:08:24,754
The champagne is here.

189
00:08:26,547 --> 00:08:28,049
- Are you okay?
- Yes, yes, I'm good.

190
00:08:28,091 --> 00:08:29,801
Are you good? Are you good?

191
00:08:29,843 --> 00:08:32,511
Is everything, are you
are y-you perfect, perfect?

192
00:08:32,553 --> 00:08:33,972
Yeah. I'm okay.

193
00:08:34,014 --> 00:08:35,098
I'm actually, I'm
a little cold, though.

194
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:36,224
Can I, can I have your jacket?

195
00:08:36,266 --> 00:08:37,684
Oh, yeah.

196
00:08:37,725 --> 00:08:41,313
Uh, no, you can't
have my jacket.

197
00:08:41,355 --> 00:08:43,940
Because then I
would be cold.

198
00:08:43,982 --> 00:08:45,692
If you thought that you
were going to be cold

199
00:08:45,733 --> 00:08:49,363
you should have brought
your own jacket.

200
00:08:49,404 --> 00:08:50,822
But, uh, other than
that are you okay?

201
00:08:50,864 --> 00:08:52,199
Are you okay?

202
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:55,702
[chuckling]
You're sure you're okay?

203
00:08:55,743 --> 00:08:58,288
Yes. I'm fine.

204
00:08:58,330 --> 00:09:01,791
In fact, I've been fine
for a long time now

205
00:09:01,833 --> 00:09:05,379
and I think...the reason is you.

206
00:09:06,338 --> 00:09:07,964
Mm, that's sweet.

207
00:09:08,006 --> 00:09:10,133
Okay, um.

208
00:09:10,175 --> 00:09:11,592
[clearing throat]

209
00:09:11,634 --> 00:09:16,515
Before I met you,
I had really little life

210
00:09:16,555 --> 00:09:19,684
and I couldn't imagine
growing old with--

211
00:09:19,726 --> 00:09:20,560
Oh, my God.

212
00:09:20,601 --> 00:09:21,936
I know, but just
let me say it.

213
00:09:21,978 --> 00:09:23,522
Oh, my God, Richard.

214
00:09:23,563 --> 00:09:25,440
What? I'm Chandler.

215
00:09:27,984 --> 00:09:29,610
Oh, that's Richard.

216
00:09:30,987 --> 00:09:32,364
Oh, God. Maybe he won't see us.

217
00:09:32,406 --> 00:09:34,074
Richard!

218
00:09:38,078 --> 00:09:39,287
Monica. Chandler.

219
00:09:39,329 --> 00:09:40,414
Hey, hey.

220
00:09:42,749 --> 00:09:45,001
I don't know
why I did that.

221
00:09:45,043 --> 00:09:46,920
Hey, it, it's good to see you.

222
00:09:46,961 --> 00:09:49,423
You, too. You let
your hair grow long.

223
00:09:49,464 --> 00:09:50,465
Yeah, oh, that's right

224
00:09:50,507 --> 00:09:52,426
you, you always
wanted me to.

225
00:09:52,467 --> 00:09:54,219
I see you got
your mustache back.

226
00:09:54,261 --> 00:09:56,721
Well, my nose got lonely.

227
00:09:56,763 --> 00:09:58,639
And, uh, you don't
have a mustache

228
00:09:58,681 --> 00:10:00,183
which is good.

229
00:10:01,768 --> 00:10:02,894
I'm Chandler, I make jokes

230
00:10:02,936 --> 00:10:05,813
when I'm uncomfortable.

231
00:10:05,855 --> 00:10:08,191
Oh, I'm sorry. Lisa.

232
00:10:08,233 --> 00:10:10,735
Monica, Chandler.
We used to date.

233
00:10:10,777 --> 00:10:13,613
Richard, no one's
supposed to know about us.

234
00:10:16,533 --> 00:10:18,285
See, I did it again.

235
00:10:19,536 --> 00:10:21,037
Chandler, w-why
don't we sit down.

236
00:10:21,079 --> 00:10:22,289
Yeah, I'll sit down.

237
00:10:22,330 --> 00:10:23,331
It's good to see you.

238
00:10:23,373 --> 00:10:24,624
Your table's ready, sir.

239
00:10:24,665 --> 00:10:27,377
- Oh. Good to see you guys.
- Yes.

240
00:10:27,419 --> 00:10:30,671
Or, if you prefer
this table's available.

241
00:10:30,713 --> 00:10:32,715
That might be fun.

242
00:10:37,387 --> 00:10:39,097
What were you thinking?

243
00:10:39,139 --> 00:10:41,099
I didn't know
it was an auction.

244
00:10:41,141 --> 00:10:43,059
I figured
I'd take a guess.

245
00:10:43,101 --> 00:10:46,271
Help a charity, free boat.

246
00:10:46,313 --> 00:10:49,065
Why would a charity
give away a free boat?

247
00:10:49,107 --> 00:10:51,276
I don't know...charity?

248
00:10:51,318 --> 00:10:52,986
Oh.

249
00:10:53,027 --> 00:10:54,779
Oh, just buy the damn boat.

250
00:10:56,573 --> 00:10:59,451
Phoebe, don't you think
you've had enough to drink?

251
00:10:59,493 --> 00:11:01,411
I'm just helping the kids.

252
00:11:03,121 --> 00:11:05,706
How is you drinking
helping the kids?

253
00:11:05,748 --> 00:11:07,501
Because the more I drink

254
00:11:07,542 --> 00:11:09,836
the less there is
for the kids to drink.

255
00:11:13,756 --> 00:11:15,509
- Mr. Tribbiani.
- Oh, hey.

256
00:11:15,550 --> 00:11:18,470
Your generous contribution
brings us a big step closer

257
00:11:18,512 --> 00:11:20,388
to building the youth center.

258
00:11:20,430 --> 00:11:21,680
Just out of curiosity

259
00:11:21,722 --> 00:11:23,141
how, how much
is that boat worth?

260
00:11:23,183 --> 00:11:25,560
I think it was valued
at $19,000.

261
00:11:25,602 --> 00:11:27,395
I was pretty close.

262
00:11:31,232 --> 00:11:33,360
Uh...so, bad news.

263
00:11:33,401 --> 00:11:35,236
Um...I can't
buy the boat.

264
00:11:35,278 --> 00:11:37,197
I don't have any money.

265
00:11:39,824 --> 00:11:40,867
[stifling laughter]

266
00:11:40,908 --> 00:11:43,662
Joey...Joey, good one.

267
00:11:43,702 --> 00:11:44,996
You're silly.

268
00:11:45,038 --> 00:11:47,541
[laughing]
Very good. Very good.

269
00:11:47,582 --> 00:11:49,501
[laughing]

270
00:11:52,003 --> 00:11:54,005
So, uh, listen I think
I'm going to take off now.

271
00:11:54,047 --> 00:11:56,424
Hey, y-you cannot
leave, Joey.

272
00:11:56,466 --> 00:11:58,259
You agreed to buy
that boat, alright?

273
00:11:58,301 --> 00:11:59,678
It is a contract.

274
00:11:59,718 --> 00:12:02,597
Plus, if you leave,
my boss is going to kill me.

275
00:12:02,639 --> 00:12:04,307
What am I going
to do, Rach?

276
00:12:04,349 --> 00:12:05,392
I don't have
that kind of money.

277
00:12:05,433 --> 00:12:07,561
I know. Okay, okay, okay, okay.

278
00:12:07,602 --> 00:12:08,936
Alright, alright. This is what
we're going to do.

279
00:12:08,978 --> 00:12:11,022
We are going to go
to the next highest bidder

280
00:12:11,064 --> 00:12:12,982
and we are just going
to let them buy it

281
00:12:13,024 --> 00:12:15,569
and then you're just
going to pay the difference.

282
00:12:15,610 --> 00:12:17,654
Okay, look, I don't know
why the kids need

283
00:12:17,696 --> 00:12:19,698
a youth center anyway, you know?

284
00:12:19,738 --> 00:12:22,450
They should just watch TV
after school, like I did

285
00:12:22,492 --> 00:12:24,077
and I turned out fine.

286
00:12:25,412 --> 00:12:27,622
Not great.

287
00:12:29,457 --> 00:12:31,209
So we're hiding
in the bathroom..

288
00:12:31,251 --> 00:12:32,669
And then I sneak out

289
00:12:32,711 --> 00:12:34,629
but, before Monica can,
her parents come in.

290
00:12:34,671 --> 00:12:36,923
Yeah, so I hide in the shower
and, the next thing you know

291
00:12:36,964 --> 00:12:39,259
they're going at it
right on the bathroom floor.

292
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:40,759
[laughing]
Oh, my God.

293
00:12:40,801 --> 00:12:42,512
I got a good one.
I got a good one.

294
00:12:42,554 --> 00:12:44,431
I once walked in on
both my parents

295
00:12:44,472 --> 00:12:46,140
making love to the same guy.

296
00:12:53,231 --> 00:12:55,358
It's so great seeing
you guys again.

297
00:12:55,400 --> 00:12:57,485
I'd like to make a toast.

298
00:12:57,527 --> 00:12:59,571
Uh...as a poet
once said

299
00:12:59,613 --> 00:13:01,656
"in the sweetness
of friendship

300
00:13:01,698 --> 00:13:03,658
"let there be laughter
and sharing of pleasures

301
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:07,036
"for in the dew
of little things

302
00:13:07,078 --> 00:13:08,329
"the heart
finds its morning

303
00:13:08,371 --> 00:13:10,248
and is refreshed."

304
00:13:10,290 --> 00:13:13,084
Oh.

305
00:13:13,126 --> 00:13:15,462
What?

306
00:13:22,218 --> 00:13:23,802
Oh, my God. You guys.

307
00:13:23,844 --> 00:13:25,096
Before you say anything

308
00:13:25,138 --> 00:13:26,765
have we got
a story for you.

309
00:13:26,805 --> 00:13:28,057
Guess who we bumped
into at dinner.

310
00:13:28,099 --> 00:13:29,808
- Who?
- Richard.

311
00:13:29,850 --> 00:13:31,852
Who? Oh.

312
00:13:31,894 --> 00:13:32,604
Oh!

313
00:13:34,230 --> 00:13:36,190
I thought you were
going out with Elizabeth.

314
00:13:36,232 --> 00:13:38,401
Yeah, I was, but she was
a little busy

315
00:13:38,443 --> 00:13:40,069
with a water
balloon fight.

316
00:13:40,111 --> 00:13:42,071
Oh, Ross, sometimes,
grownups have commitments

317
00:13:42,113 --> 00:13:44,865
they just can't get out of.

318
00:13:44,907 --> 00:13:47,160
You know, maybe she 
is too young for me.

319
00:13:47,201 --> 00:13:48,536
When I was over there
and she was

320
00:13:48,578 --> 00:13:49,746
running around with her friends

321
00:13:49,788 --> 00:13:51,665
I felt like I was a baby-sitter.

322
00:13:51,706 --> 00:13:52,999
I finally started to see

323
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:54,875
what you guys
were talking about.

324
00:13:54,917 --> 00:13:56,085
I don't know what to do.

325
00:13:56,127 --> 00:13:57,253
Why don't you
just weigh out

326
00:13:57,295 --> 00:13:58,463
the good stuff about
the relationship

327
00:13:58,505 --> 00:13:59,838
against the bad stuff?

328
00:13:59,880 --> 00:14:02,300
I mean that's what I did
when I first started..

329
00:14:02,342 --> 00:14:05,219
...weighing stuff.

330
00:14:05,261 --> 00:14:07,597
Okay, bad stuff:

331
00:14:07,639 --> 00:14:10,099
Well, I'm-I'm 12 years older
than she is.

332
00:14:10,141 --> 00:14:12,226
If the school finds
out, you're fired.

333
00:14:12,268 --> 00:14:13,520
She's leaving
for three months.

334
00:14:13,561 --> 00:14:14,937
For camp.

335
00:14:16,606 --> 00:14:18,149
Okay, good stuff.

336
00:14:18,191 --> 00:14:22,236
Um...well, she's...she's
sweet and pretty and..

337
00:14:22,278 --> 00:14:24,155
Look, Ross, the only
question you need to ask

338
00:14:24,197 --> 00:14:26,449
is do you see a future?

339
00:14:26,491 --> 00:14:28,535
I mean, like, do you see
yourself marrying her?

340
00:14:29,952 --> 00:14:31,078
Oh, my God,
you did it already.

341
00:14:31,120 --> 00:14:32,831
You married her,
didn't you?

342
00:14:34,708 --> 00:14:36,876
No, no, I didn't do that.

343
00:14:36,917 --> 00:14:39,212
It's just..

344
00:14:39,253 --> 00:14:40,921
Okay, honestly, no.

345
00:14:40,963 --> 00:14:44,843
I don't...I don't see
a big future with her.

346
00:14:44,884 --> 00:14:47,846
Okay, well, I-I think
that's your answer.

347
00:14:47,887 --> 00:14:49,096
I got to talk to her.

348
00:14:49,138 --> 00:14:50,765
Oh, I hate this part.

349
00:14:50,807 --> 00:14:52,475
You have to forget
about Elizabeth.

350
00:14:52,517 --> 00:14:53,768
I mean, if you're not careful

351
00:14:53,810 --> 00:14:56,062
you may not get married
at all this year.

352
00:15:00,525 --> 00:15:02,026
Rachel, Rachel.

353
00:15:02,068 --> 00:15:03,653
Okay, the next highest bidder
is at table one.

354
00:15:03,695 --> 00:15:05,154
- Oh, great.
- Yeah.

355
00:15:05,196 --> 00:15:07,198
Oh, and the guy who got the
Paris trip is at table four.

356
00:15:07,240 --> 00:15:08,032
Oh, okay.

357
00:15:08,074 --> 00:15:09,200
Why do you care about the guy

358
00:15:09,242 --> 00:15:10,243
who won the Paris trip?

359
00:15:10,284 --> 00:15:12,704
It's a trip for two.

360
00:15:12,746 --> 00:15:15,081
Excuse me.

361
00:15:19,627 --> 00:15:23,506
Excuse me, is the person who won
the Paris trip at this table?

362
00:15:23,548 --> 00:15:26,050
- That was me.
- Oh. Enchante.

363
00:15:29,888 --> 00:15:31,180
Uh...excuse me.

364
00:15:31,222 --> 00:15:33,057
Is there a Mr. Bowmont
at this table?

365
00:15:33,099 --> 00:15:35,101
- That's me.
- Oh, yeah.

366
00:15:35,142 --> 00:15:36,728
Well, hello.

367
00:15:36,770 --> 00:15:39,439
This is your
lucky day, Mr. Bowmont.

368
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:41,733
The gentleman daysailer

369
00:15:41,775 --> 00:15:43,568
has just become
available again.

370
00:15:43,610 --> 00:15:46,738
And I believe that you made
a bid of $18,000.

371
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:48,698
Y-You have to pay that.
It's not just a guess.

372
00:15:48,740 --> 00:15:49,908
Okay.

373
00:15:53,244 --> 00:15:54,995
I was actually
relieved, uh

374
00:15:55,037 --> 00:15:56,080
I didn't win the boat.

375
00:15:56,122 --> 00:15:57,582
My wife would have killed me.

376
00:15:57,624 --> 00:15:59,626
Are you kidding me?
She's going to love this boat.

377
00:15:59,667 --> 00:16:02,754
- Yeah. What's your wife's name?
- It's Pam.

378
00:16:02,796 --> 00:16:07,216
Pam. Oh, God. Just imagine
this, the Pam.

379
00:16:07,258 --> 00:16:08,968
Oh...ho-ho.

380
00:16:10,177 --> 00:16:12,722
I don't think she'd like that.

381
00:16:12,764 --> 00:16:15,892
Okay, i-imagine this,
the Mr. Bowmont.

382
00:16:15,934 --> 00:16:17,518
O-oh.

383
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:18,895
I don't think so, dear.

384
00:16:18,937 --> 00:16:21,188
Okay, look, let me paint
you a little picture.

385
00:16:21,230 --> 00:16:23,942
Alright, you are
setting sail up the Hudson.

386
00:16:23,983 --> 00:16:28,655
You got the wind
in your ha-arms.

387
00:16:28,696 --> 00:16:31,073
You, you get all
the peace and quiet

388
00:16:31,115 --> 00:16:32,867
that you've always wanted.

389
00:16:32,909 --> 00:16:34,535
You get back to nature.

390
00:16:34,577 --> 00:16:36,078
You can go fishing.

391
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:37,705
You c-can get one
of those little hats

392
00:16:37,747 --> 00:16:39,833
and have people
call you "captain."

393
00:16:39,874 --> 00:16:41,793
And then, when you're old,
"cappy."

394
00:16:44,295 --> 00:16:46,380
What the hell. It's for
a good cause. Alright.

395
00:16:46,422 --> 00:16:48,424
No way. It's mine.

396
00:16:48,466 --> 00:16:51,803
What? What?

397
00:16:51,845 --> 00:16:54,555
All that stuff you
just said, I want that!

398
00:16:54,597 --> 00:16:56,641
Joey, you don't
have $20,000.

399
00:16:56,683 --> 00:16:58,100
Who cares?
I'll make payments.

400
00:16:58,142 --> 00:17:00,394
Whatever it takes.
I want the Mr. Bowmont.

401
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:08,027
Oh, my God, you're back.

402
00:17:08,068 --> 00:17:11,447
Oh, let me see it.
Let me see your hand.

403
00:17:11,489 --> 00:17:13,157
Why do you want
to see my hand?

404
00:17:13,199 --> 00:17:14,659
I want to see
what's in your hand.

405
00:17:14,701 --> 00:17:16,870
I want to see the trash.

406
00:17:19,246 --> 00:17:20,915
Ew-w. O-oh,
it's all dirty.

407
00:17:20,957 --> 00:17:22,876
- You should throw this out.
- Yeah.

408
00:17:24,210 --> 00:17:25,545
Okay.

409
00:17:28,339 --> 00:17:29,716
What did you
guys just do?

410
00:17:29,757 --> 00:17:30,925
What happened?

411
00:17:30,967 --> 00:17:32,635
Richard was there,
so I couldn't do it.

412
00:17:32,677 --> 00:17:34,637
- Oh, no.
- Oh.

413
00:17:34,679 --> 00:17:37,598
I was going to do it tomorrow,
you know, and surprise her.

414
00:17:37,640 --> 00:17:40,100
- But now you've ruined it.
- Oh, we didn't ruin it.

415
00:17:40,142 --> 00:17:43,270
Who walks into a room and asks
to see a person's hands?

416
00:17:43,312 --> 00:17:46,190
Well, a palm reader,
a manicurist

417
00:17:46,983 --> 00:17:48,651
a hand doctor.

418
00:17:48,693 --> 00:17:50,069
Glove salesman.

419
00:17:50,110 --> 00:17:51,905
Good one. Yeah.

420
00:17:53,698 --> 00:17:55,700
This is terrible.
What am I going to do?

421
00:17:55,742 --> 00:17:57,618
Look, she only suspects
something, okay?

422
00:17:57,660 --> 00:17:58,870
She doesn't know for sure

423
00:17:58,912 --> 00:18:00,413
so just throw her
off the track.

424
00:18:00,454 --> 00:18:01,706
That's right. I could
throw her off.

425
00:18:01,748 --> 00:18:03,041
I could make her think that, uh

426
00:18:03,081 --> 00:18:04,667
marriage was the last thing
on my mind.

427
00:18:04,709 --> 00:18:07,086
Yeah, convince her t-that
you're scared of commitment.

428
00:18:07,127 --> 00:18:09,964
I can do that.
I've had 30 years of practice.

429
00:18:10,006 --> 00:18:12,592
Hey, being you
is finally going to pay off.

430
00:18:14,343 --> 00:18:16,136
Oh, I had to go all the way
to the basement

431
00:18:16,178 --> 00:18:18,014
because some idiot keeps
stuffing the trash chute

432
00:18:18,056 --> 00:18:20,641
with pizza boxes.

433
00:18:20,683 --> 00:18:22,936
That guy still doing that? Huh.

434
00:18:24,854 --> 00:18:26,814
Hey. Oh, my God, you're here.

435
00:18:26,856 --> 00:18:28,733
- Let me see your hand.
- No, you're too late.

436
00:18:28,775 --> 00:18:30,651
She already took
out the trash.

437
00:18:39,368 --> 00:18:40,536
[Ross thinking] Wow.

438
00:18:40,578 --> 00:18:42,329
I have never had 

439
00:18:42,371 --> 00:18:44,415
such a healthy breakup.

440
00:18:44,457 --> 00:18:46,333
She was such

441
00:18:46,375 --> 00:18:48,210
a grownup about it.

442
00:18:48,252 --> 00:18:50,129
She didn't seem too

443
00:18:50,170 --> 00:18:53,049
immature for me.

444
00:18:53,091 --> 00:18:56,427
Did I just make a huge mistake?

445
00:18:56,469 --> 00:18:58,471
Ross, wait.

446
00:18:58,512 --> 00:19:00,431
Elizabeth...thank God.

447
00:19:00,473 --> 00:19:01,808
I was just thinking about--

448
00:19:01,849 --> 00:19:04,226
You suck!

449
00:19:04,268 --> 00:19:05,436
What?

450
00:19:11,400 --> 00:19:13,111
Okay.

451
00:19:13,152 --> 00:19:15,029
Breakup's still on.

452
00:19:23,997 --> 00:19:25,665
Okay, okay, here she comes.
How do I look?

453
00:19:25,706 --> 00:19:28,084
Do I look like a guy
who doesn't want to get married?

454
00:19:28,126 --> 00:19:32,130
Yeah. And also
a little like a French guy.

455
00:19:35,424 --> 00:19:37,426
I never noticed
that before.

456
00:19:38,928 --> 00:19:39,929
Hey, guys.

457
00:19:39,971 --> 00:19:41,305
- Hey.
- Hey.

458
00:19:41,347 --> 00:19:43,182
- What are you up to?
- Oh, just hanging out.

459
00:19:43,223 --> 00:19:45,184
Talking about websites.

460
00:19:45,225 --> 00:19:47,561
Yeah.

461
00:19:47,603 --> 00:19:49,522
Yeah, we saw this really
interesting website

462
00:19:49,563 --> 00:19:52,859
about marriage and how totally
unnecessary it is

463
00:19:52,900 --> 00:19:55,903
and how it's just a way for the
government to keep tabs on you.

464
00:19:55,945 --> 00:19:59,115
Yeah. Big brother.

465
00:19:59,157 --> 00:20:00,658
Well, that's a little crazy.

466
00:20:00,700 --> 00:20:02,242
Although I am,
you know, glad to hear

467
00:20:02,284 --> 00:20:03,327
that you're branching out
on what you

468
00:20:03,369 --> 00:20:05,788
look at on the internet.

469
00:20:05,830 --> 00:20:07,373
You know, it just
got me thinking, though.

470
00:20:07,414 --> 00:20:10,001
Why would anybody ever
want to get married, huh?

471
00:20:10,043 --> 00:20:12,211
Why? To celebrate
your relationship.

472
00:20:12,252 --> 00:20:14,296
To solidify your commitment.

473
00:20:14,338 --> 00:20:18,216
To declare your love for one
another to the world.

474
00:20:18,258 --> 00:20:21,137
Eh.

475
00:20:21,179 --> 00:20:23,347
Okay, well,
that's good to know.

476
00:20:25,141 --> 00:20:27,977
Ah. The Mr. Bowmont's here!

477
00:20:34,192 --> 00:20:35,693
Hey, Monica, there's a
customer who wants

478
00:20:35,735 --> 00:20:37,277
to compliment the chef.

479
00:20:37,319 --> 00:20:39,321
- Shall I let him in?
- Sure. I love this part.

480
00:20:40,948 --> 00:20:42,491
Come on in.

481
00:20:42,533 --> 00:20:44,118
Hi.

482
00:20:44,160 --> 00:20:45,703
Richard.

483
00:20:45,745 --> 00:20:48,164
Actually, I'm not here
to compliment the chef.

484
00:20:48,206 --> 00:20:50,041
Oh, that's okay.

485
00:20:50,083 --> 00:20:51,792
I hate when people come back
to compliment the chef.

486
00:20:51,834 --> 00:20:53,335
Like I have
nothing better to do.

487
00:20:55,337 --> 00:20:56,881
So what's up?

488
00:20:56,923 --> 00:20:59,675
Well, it was great seeing you
the other night.

489
00:20:59,717 --> 00:21:01,219
Yeah, it was good
to see you, too.

490
00:21:03,387 --> 00:21:05,139
Did you come down here
to tell me that?

491
00:21:05,181 --> 00:21:07,767
No. I came here
to tell you something else.

492
00:21:10,228 --> 00:21:13,606
I came here
to tell you I still love you.

493
00:21:21,781 --> 00:21:24,700
[theme music]

